## §1 The question under test

SAEOS-1 §9/A8 carries the specification's self-declared **single largest untested
assumption**:

> **A8** — "Ω1 (self-population) is presented as a breakthrough but assumes PAEOS emits
> clean structured decisions. Does it?" **[MODIFY]** Partially. PAEOS *produces* all the
> required artifacts (L06 matrices, L08 design sets, L11 contracts, decision-graph
> records, scars), but as prose documents, not as typed emission bundles. Slice S1 exists
> precisely to build and test that extraction. **Carried:** if L15 artifacts prove too
> unstructured to extract losslessly, S1 fails and Ω1's value is overstated.

This probe attempts the extraction **by hand**, before any code, against three real sealed
goals, and reports the measured loss. §8 of SAEOS-1 sets S1's exit criterion as
"round-trip is **lossless**"; the purpose here is to find out whether that criterion is
attainable and, if not, by how much it is missed and *why*.

**Headline result:** A8 is falsified, but not in the direction it predicted. The problem
is **not** that PAEOS's artifacts are too unstructured to parse. The problem is that the
artifacts SAEOS-1 §6.1b names as its inputs — the **L06 trade-off matrix** and
**hash-bound evidence** — **do not exist at all**, for any sealed goal in the corpus,
while a large, uniformly machine-structured source that §6.1b never names (the autonomous
friction ledger) does. Ω1's claim that "PAEOS already emits exactly what SAKG needs" is
**false as stated**; PAEOS emits roughly two-thirds of it, and the missing third is a
producer-side conformance gap, not an extraction problem.

