## §7 Why the loss occurs — four root causes

A8 predicted the failure mode would be *"prose, not typed bundles"* — a **formatting**
problem. It is not. Every finding below is an **absence** or a **routing** problem, which
is a different class of defect with a different fix.

### §7.1 The L06 trade-off matrix does not exist — and 88% of goals were required to produce one

`ENGINEERING_LIFECYCLE.md` L06 mandates a "trade-off matrix (axes × approaches)" as a
required artifact, and names it the "Judge's selection basis". SAEOS-1 §6.1b names it as
the source of every `TradeOff` node.

- `grep -rli 'trade-?off' reviews/ docs/ architecture/ design/ ledger/` → **0 matrix
  artifacts anywhere in the readable corpus.**
- Phase II (L03–L11) auto-discharges for **Trace-A** work only. Per the B0.11 classifier
  rule, any diff touching `kernel/` or `constitution/` is **HARD**; `ENGINEERING_LIFECYCLE.md:228`
  makes kernel-surface self-application **Trace-B**, in which "all 19 states execute in
  full".
- **37 of 42** sealed-goal reviews reference `kernel/` or `constitution/`.

So ~88% of sealed goals were constitutionally required to run L06 in full and none
produced its required artifact. **This is a PAEOS lifecycle-conformance gap, not a SAKG
extraction problem** — and it is worth fixing whether or not SAKG is ever built. It is
also the reason `TradeOff` scores 0.17: you cannot extract what was never written.

### §7.2 Two L17 review templates are mandated at the same path, and the mandated one is the poorer

- `operations/ENGINEERING_LIFECYCLE.md:206` (L17) mandates the **CER-4 six questions**
  (a)–(f) → output to `reviews/tasks/<task_id>_review.md`.
- `operations/IMPLEMENTATION_REVIEW_PROTOCOL.md` §5 mandates a **10-point PASS/FAIL
  checklist** → the same path.

In practice the six-questions form landed in `docs/*-constitutional-review.md` (4 goals)
and the checklist form in `reviews/tasks/` (42 goals). The checklist form is where nearly
all the extractable content **is not**: it drops the adversary pass, the invalidated
assumptions, the rejected alternatives, and the founder verdict. The most complete Scar in
the corpus (B0.2's orphaned `.tmp`) exists only in the `docs/` twin; the `reviews/tasks/`
twin for the same goal says "Zero technical debt."

**Consequence for S1: the emitter must not read `reviews/tasks/`.** Targeting the
constitutionally-named path would have produced the 0.44 fidelity, and the probe would
have returned NO-GO.

### §7.3 SAEOS-1 §6.1b does not name the corpus's single best-structured source

**128 of 176** debt records are on a machine-generated friction template
(`## 1. Observation` / `## 2. Leverage Assessment` / `## 3. Confidence Assessment` /
`## 4. Root Cause`), each carrying an **explicit goal id** (`goal \`g-...\``) and
**implicated paths**. This is already typed, already uniform, already joined to the goal
graph, and already emitted autonomously by the RB-0008 friction handler.

It maps directly onto `Scar` (`root_cause`, `detection_signature` = friction class +
implicated paths) and onto `Outcome` for non-seal terminations. §6.1b's emission list
(`Decision`, `TradeOff`, `Option`, `Constraint`, `Component`, `Scar`, `EvidenceRef`) never
mentions it. **This is free yield the specification is leaving on the table** — and the
only large machine-structured source in the corpus.

Caveat, recorded honestly: `guard_action` is absent from the friction template ("Root
Cause: To be investigated"), so these become **incomplete** Scars until characterized.

### §7.4 The emission point is off by one lifecycle stage

SAEOS-1 §6.1b places the emitter at **L15** ("L15 emits a SAKG Emission Bundle"), fired
when "a PAEOS goal reaches L16 (Promotion) and seals". But:

- **L15** (Documentation & Ledger) produces "updated Inventory Atlas, roadmaps,
  architecture diagrams, knowledge graph, decision logs".
- **L17** (Retrospective + Constitutional Review) produces the Constitutional Review —
  the artifact carrying the invalidated assumptions, the adversary pass, the debt
  records, and the founder verdict. **Every high-scoring cell in this probe comes from an
  L17 or CER-3 artifact, not an L15 one.**

An emitter wired to L15 fires **before the richest artifact is written**. This is a
one-line correction to §6.1b, but it is load-bearing: at L15 the probe's fidelity would
drop to approximately the §6.3 floor.

